  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 212 PM EDT FRI SEP 25 2026\n\nEastern Kentucky will begin the period on the far outer fringes \nof a large mature low pressure system along or just offshore the \nMid-Atlantic seaboard, with surface high pressure ridging \nextending south from a surface high in southern Canada. This \nsystem will eventually lift northeast early next week, with weak \nridging building over the area. Complexity in the forecast \nincreases significantly toward the latter half of next week as \nmodels struggle on the timing, location, and strength of the next \nupper low/trough that moves into the middle of the country, which \nwill eventually bring the next chance of precipitation. \n\nConfidence is high in the temperature trends through the long term \nperiod, with cool and dry conditions through this weekend giving way \nto an increasingly warmer and more humid regime as the upper flow \ntransitions from a northerly to more southwesterly direction. By the \nmiddle of next week, highs should be returning to above normal, \nthough to what degree is somewhat uncertain, with NBM deterministic \nvalues in the upper 70s to lower 80s for Wednesday and Thursday, \nwhich is on the lower end of the probabilistic envelope. Lows will \nalso increase from the 40s for lows this weekend to the 50s and 60s \nby the second half of next week. Confidence is also low as to the \ntiming of arrival of possible precipitation associated with the next \nsystem moving into the center of the country next week, with low \nchances (generally around 15 to 25 percent) introduced to the grids \nbeginning Wednesday night and gradually increasing (to 15 to \n35 percent) through the end of the long-term period Thursday\nnight.",
